class heightchecker {

    public int heightChecker(int[] heights) {

        int expected[] = heights.clone();
        Arrays.sort(expected);

        int count =0;
        for(int i=0; i<heights.length; i++) {
            if(heights[i] != expected[i]) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
        
        }
}



//Sorting Approach (Your current solution)
Code logic recap:
Copy array → O(n)
Sort array → O(n log n)
Compare arrays → O(n)
⏱️ Time Complexity
👉 Overall:
O(n log n)
👉 Why?
Sorting dominates everything.
Java uses Dual-Pivot QuickSort for primitives → average O(n log n)
📌 Example:
n = 6
heights = [1,1,4,2,1,3]

Sorting steps roughly:

Split → Merge → Compare

Even if comparisons are fast, sorting still takes:

≈ n log n = 6 * log(6) ≈ 6 * 2.5 ≈ 15 operations

Then:

Comparison loop = 6 operations

👉 Total ≈ 21 operations → dominated by sorting

📦 Space Complexity
O(n)
👉 Why?
You create a new array:
int[] expected = heights.clone();

This stores n elements → extra memory

✅ 2. Optimized Approach (Counting Sort)

Since:

1 <= heights[i] <= 100

👉 Range is small → we can avoid sorting

⏱️ Time Complexity
O(n + k)

Where:

n = number of students
k = range of values (max height = 100)

👉 So:

O(n + 100) ≈ O(n)
📌 Why?

Steps:

Count frequencies → O(n)
Rebuild sorted order → O(n)
Compare → O(n)

Total:

O(n + n + n) = O(n)
📦 Space Complexity
O(1)
👉 Why?
Count array size is fixed:
int[] count = new int[101];

👉 Always 101 → constant space

📌 Example (Same Input)
heights = [1,1,4,2,1,3]
Step 1: Count
count[1] = 3
count[2] = 1
count[3] = 1
count[4] = 1
Step 2: Build expected order
[1,1,1,2,3,4]
Step 3: Compare → mismatches = 3
🔥 Final Comparison
Approach	Time Complexity	Space Complexity	When to Use
Sorting	O(n log n)	O(n)	General case
Counting Sort	O(n)	O(1)	When range is small
💡 Interview Tip

If interviewer sees constraint:

heights[i] <= 100

👉 They EXPECT you to say:

